# The maximal abelian subextension and the abelianisation: a missing hypothesis

This module contains no mathematics that is used elsewhere: it is documentation, in the
form of docstrings, of (a) why the statement

  `NK.LocalInputs.AbelianRank.hasAbelianFrattiniRank_of_maxAbelian`

  `HasFrattiniRank p r (maxAbelianSubext κ K' ≃ₐ[κ] maxAbelianSubext κ K')`
      `→ HasAbelianFrattiniRank p r (Gal(K'/κ))`

is **false as written** for an arbitrary Galois extension `κ ⊆ K'`, and (b) the exact
extra hypothesis under which it becomes true, with its proof route.  Compare the sibling
convention already used in `Submission/Work/LocalAnabelian/PadicField/FrattiniRank.lean`,
`Submission/Work/LocalAnabelian/GaloisCorrespondence/IndexP.lean` and
`Submission/Work/LocalAnabelian/GaloisCorrespondence/numIndexSubgroups_abelianization_eq_natCard_galois.lean`,
which all carry a closedness/openness hypothesis of exactly this kind.

## 1. The two groups being compared

Let `κ ⊆ K'` be Galois, `G := Gal(K'/κ) = (K' ≃ₐ[κ] K')`, `S := commutator G` (the
*algebraic* commutator subgroup, `Subgroup.closure` of the commutators — **not** closed
in the Krull topology in general), and

  `A := maxAbelianSubext κ K' = IntermediateField.fixedField S`.

Then `A/κ` is Galois and the restriction map
`AlgEquiv.restrictNormalHom A : G →* (A ≃ₐ[κ] A)` is surjective
(`AlgEquiv.restrictNormalHom_surjective`) with kernel `A.fixingSubgroup`
(`IntermediateField.restrictNormalHom_ker`), so

  `(A ≃ₐ[κ] A) ≃* G ⧸ A.fixingSubgroup`.

The key structural point is

  `A.fixingSubgroup = S.topologicalClosure`   (closure in the Krull topology),

i.e. the hypothesis `HasFrattiniRank p r (A ≃ₐ[κ] A)` is a statement about the
**topological** abelianisation `G ⧸ S.topologicalClosure` of `G`, whereas the conclusion
`HasAbelianFrattiniRank p r G := HasFrattiniRank p r (Abelianization G)` is a statement
about the **abstract** abelianisation `G ⧸ S` (`Abelianization G = G ⧸ S`, by definition).
So the lemma asserts

  `numIndexSubgroups p (G ⧸ S.topologicalClosure) = numIndexSubgroups p (G ⧸ S)`,

and the natural surjection `G ⧸ S ↠ G ⧸ S.topologicalClosure` is *not* injective in
general.

Both `numIndexSubgroups p` are counts of subgroups of index `p` of the corresponding
quotient, i.e. (via the correspondence theorem for a quotient by `N \trianglelefteq G`)
of subgroups `M` of `G` with `N ≤ M` and `[G : M] = p`.  Hence the statement is
equivalent to the equality of the two sets

  `{M ≤ G // S ≤ M ∧ M.index = p}`   and   `{M ≤ G // S.topologicalClosure ≤ M ∧ M.index = p}`.

The first contains the second.  For the converse one must show that an index-`p` subgroup
`M ⊇ S` is *closed*, i.e. `M ⊇ S.topologicalClosure`.  This is exactly what fails in
general: such an `M` is automatically normal (for `g ∈ G`, `m ∈ M` one has
`g m g⁻¹ = m * ⁅m, g⁻¹⁆ ∈ M` because `⁅m, g⁻¹⁆ ∈ S ≤ M`), so the missing input is that
**index-`p` normal subgroups of `Gal(K'/κ)` are open (equivalently closed)**.

That input is *not* automatic — see §2 — but it *does* hold in the intended application:
for `κ = K_𝔭` a `p`-adic completion, `Gal(κ̄/κ)` is topologically finitely generated, so
every finite-index subgroup is open (Nikolov–Segal).  This is the same hypothesis that
`NK.LocalAnabelian.PadicField.FrattiniRank.lean` already carries explicitly.

## 2. Why the unconditional statement is false: a counterexample

Non-closed finite-index subgroups of Galois groups really occur.  Take the pro-`2` group

  `G := P ⋊ C`, `P := ∏_{n ∈ ℤ} ℤ/2`, `C := ⟨σ⟩ ≅ ℤ/2`, `σ·e_n = e_{-n}`,

which is topologically generated by countably many elements, hence a quotient of the free
profinite group `F̂(ℵ₀)`.  Free profinite groups are projective, and by Ax (1968) and
Lubotzky–van den Dries (1981) *a profinite group is projective if and only if it is the
absolute Galois group of a pseudo-algebraically closed field*; taking a PAC field `κ` of
characteristic `0` with `G_κ ≅ F̂(ℵ₀)` and `K' := (κ̄)^{ker}` realises `G` as `Gal(K'/κ)`
(quotients of Galois groups are Galois groups).  In `G`:

* a direct computation of the commutators `(a, σ^i)(b, σ^j)` gives
  `S = [G,G] = span_{𝔽₂}{e_n + e_{-n} : n ∈ ℤ}`, the *finitely supported* `σ`-invariant
  vectors;
* hence `S.topologicalClosure = P^σ = {v ∈ P // v_n = v_{-n} ∀ n}`, of index `2` in `P`
  and index `4` in `G`, so `A/κ` has group `G ⧸ P^σ ≅ (ℤ/2)²`;
* thus `numIndexSubgroups 2 (A ≃ₐ[κ] A) = 3 = (2² - 1)/(2 - 1)`, i.e. the hypothesis
  `HasFrattiniRank 2 2 (A ≃ₐ[κ] A)` **holds**;
* but `Abelianization G = (P ⧸ S) × C` with `P ⧸ S` infinite-dimensional over `𝔽₂` (the
  `σ`-invariant functionals `ψ_n(e_m) = δ_{mn} + δ_{m,-n}`, `n ≥ 1`, all kill `S` and are
  independent), so `Abelianization G` has infinitely many index-`2` subgroups and
  `numIndexSubgroups 2 (Abelianization G) = 0` (the `Nat.card` of an infinite type),
  whereas the conclusion demands `3`.  The conclusion is **false**.

The same phenomenon can be seen over `ℚ`: `G_ℚ^{ab} = ∏_p ℤ_p^×` has index-`2` subgroups
that are not open (a non-principal ultrafilter on the primes gives a non-continuous
functional `∏_p ℤ/2 → ℤ/2`), so not every finite-index subgroup of `Gal(ℚ̄/ℚ)` is open.

## 3. The corrected statement, and its proof route

**Status: this section is not just a route, it is proved.**  The corrected statement is
`NK.LocalInputs.AbelianRank.hasAbelianFrattiniRank_of_maxAbelian_of_isOpen` in the sibling
file `hasAbelianFrattiniRank_of_maxAbelian_of_isOpen.lean`, which also contains the two
reusable ingredients `numIndexSubgroups_eq_natCard_comap` and
`numIndexSubgroups_quotient_commutator_eq_topologicalClosure`.  The structural
Galois-theoretic identity used there (step 1 below) is
`NK.LocalInputs.AbelianRank.fixingSubgroup_fixedField_eq_topologicalClosure` in
`fixingSubgroup_fixedField_eq_topologicalClosure.lean`.  All are sorry-free.

Add the openness hypothesis

  `hopen : ∀ H : Subgroup (Gal(K'/κ)), H.index = p → H.Normal → IsOpen (H : Set (Gal(K'/κ)))`

(the form used in `NK.LocalAnabelian.PadicField.FrattiniRank.lean`; it is satisfied for
the absolute Galois group of a `p`-adic field by Nikolov–Segal).  Then the transfer
*is* a theorem, and the proof decomposes as follows.

* **Step 1 (the fixing subgroup of the maximal abelian subextension).**  For *any*
  subgroup `H ≤ Gal(E/F)` of a Galois extension,
  `(IntermediateField.fixedField H).fixingSubgroup = H.topologicalClosure`.
  Proof: `H ≤ (fixedField H).fixingSubgroup` (`IntermediateField.le_iff_le`), and the
  right-hand side is closed (`InfiniteGalois.fixingSubgroup_isClosed`), giving
  `≥` by `Subgroup.topologicalClosure_minimal`; for `≤` use
  `H ≤ H.topologicalClosure`, hence `fixedField H.topologicalClosure ≤ fixedField H`
  (`IntermediateField.fixedField_le`), hence by contravariance
  `(fixedField H).fixingSubgroup ≤ (fixedField H.topologicalClosure).fixingSubgroup
  = H.topologicalClosure`, the last equality being
  `InfiniteGalois.fixingSubgroup_fixedField` applied to the closed subgroup
  `H.topologicalClosure`.  In particular `A.fixingSubgroup = S.topologicalClosure`.

* **Step 2 (the quotient is the Galois group of `A`).**  `AlgEquiv.restrictNormalHom A` is
  surjective with kernel `A.fixingSubgroup`, so it induces
  `G ⧸ A.fixingSubgroup ≃* (A ≃ₐ[κ] A)` (`MonoidHom.quotientKerEquivOfSurjective`), and
  `numIndexSubgroups p` transports along this isomorphism
  (`NK.LocalAnabelian.numIndexSubgroups_congr`).

* **Step 3 (the two counts agree).**  For a normal subgroup `N \trianglelefteq G`, the
  subgroups of index `p` of `G ⧸ N` correspond to the subgroups `M` of `G` with `N ≤ M`
  and `M.index = p` (correspondence theorem, with `Subgroup.index_map_eq` and
  `Subgroup.index_comap_of_surjective` to see that the index is preserved).  For
  `N = S` the corresponding set is `{M // S ≤ M ∧ M.index = p}`, for
  `N = S.topologicalClosure` it is `{M // S.topologicalClosure ≤ M ∧ M.index = p}`;
  the second is contained in the first, and conversely if `S ≤ M` with
  `M.index = p` then `M` is normal (as computed in §1), so `M` is open by `hopen` and
  hence closed (an open subgroup of finite index has open complement, being the union of
  its finitely many other cosets), so `M ⊇ S.topologicalClosure`.  Hence the two sets are
  equal, the two `Nat.card`s are equal, and the conclusion follows.
-/
